00:02:59.681 --> 00:03:00.180
All right.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.520
So as Frances said,
I'm going to talk

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.740
through a little bit of
the history of Apache

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.840
Beam from Google's perspective.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.280
Specifically, I'm going to
give a brief history of four

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.980
projects that led up
to the creation of all

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.170
the technology that's in Beam.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.600
And I'm also going to talk
through Google's motivation

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.610
for being a part of the creation
of the Beam project itself.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:25.420
So first up, a brief history
of data processing at Google--

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:29.760
so our journey with large
scale data processing

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.490
began with MapReduce
back in 2003.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.860
So before MapReduce,
anyone within Google,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.060
or without really, who wanted
to perform large scale data

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.460
processing had to
create a custom system.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.590
This custom system
consisted of two things.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.900
It consisted of the
data processing logic--

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.970
so the actual code that
described the type data

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.560
processing you
wanted to perform--

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.909
as well as this complicated
set of distributed systems

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:53.700
logic to give you the
scalability and fault

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.475
tolerance over unreliable
machines that you needed.

00:03:56.475 --> 00:03:58.350
And this was a real
hassle, because everybody

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.690
was constantly rebuilding
these custom systems

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.940
and dealing with both
their data processing

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.854
logic and their
distributing systems logic.

00:04:04.854 --> 00:04:07.020
And so what the folks who
created MapReduce realized

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.906
was, if we could
split those two apart,

00:04:09.906 --> 00:04:11.280
it would make
lives a lot easier.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.900
And so that's what
MapReduce did.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.800
It really provided a simple
API for describing your data

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.630
processing logic.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.839
And then that was coupled
with a scalable fault tolerant

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.275
execution engine for running it.

00:04:23.275 --> 00:04:24.900
And this was very
successful at Google.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.790
And we thought it was so
great that we really wanted

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:27.915
to share it with the world.

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:31.470
So we wrote a paper.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.040
As MapReduce continued to
be successful within Google,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.600
some common patterns evolved.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.280
People wanted to solve more
complex data processing

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.950
problems than you could express
with just a single MapReduce,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.560
so they would write large
pipelines of MapReduces.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.050
You'd have these sequences
and trees of MapReduces.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.120
And since they had all
these MapReduces lined up

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.786
that needed to be
run, everybody started

00:04:52.786 --> 00:04:55.560
writing custom systems to
orchestrate the execution

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:56.760
of these MapReduces.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.520
And additionally, to make
these run really well--

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.800
really perform it
really efficiently,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.540
they started hand
tuning the code.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.504
So they'd take this
nice, clean logical code

00:05:05.504 --> 00:05:07.920
that they'd written, and they'd
start pulling pieces of it

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.110
out into other MapReduces
and performing these hand

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.820
optimizations that
would obfuscate what

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.320
the actual logic
was supposed to be,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.180
but make the
MapReduces run faster.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.360
So FlumeJava aimed
to change that.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.950
And it did so in three ways.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:24.060
One is that it expanded
the API for MapReduce

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.440
and generalized
it into something

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.040
that could express an
arbitrarily complex and large

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.840
pipeline in a
clean, clear manner.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.040
Additionally, it was able to
take this clearly expressed

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.140
pipeline and optimize
it under the covers

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.110
and come up with an efficient
and performant execution

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.960
plan for actually running
this thing on real hardware.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.320
And then additionally,
it provided orchestration

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.950
of these various MapReduces
that were actually

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.050
the physical
representation of the code

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.890
that users had written.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.010
And as before, this
worked out really well.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.640
It was very successful
Google, and we desperately

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.389
wanted to share it
with the outside world.

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:01.170
So we wrote a paper.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.050
Around the same time,
there was a class

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.620
of problems that were being
solved with MapReduce and Flume

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.780
but weren't really
being served well by it.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.060
These were the types
of problems that

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.940
dealt with unbounded data-- so
essentially, infinite streaming

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.850
data sets that needed to
be processed continuously--

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.190
and also typically
really benefited

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.490
from providing low latency
results-- so getting answers

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.734
quickly.

00:06:24.734 --> 00:06:26.400
And so the MillWheel
project was created

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.530
to try to address
those use cases.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:32.010
And what it brought to the table
was a true streaming engine

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.620
that could operate on the
level of record by record.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.800
So as data flow
in, one record at

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.440
a time, it could process it
and give you your results.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.110
And in doing so, this was able
to provide those low latency

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:46.020
results that folks were wanting
over unbounded data sets.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.860
But it also did so without
compromising correctness.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.750
And this was even
in light of things

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.210
like machine failures,
fault tolerance,

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.460
and out of order data.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.430
And as before-- as the trend
you've probably seen so far--

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.040
we really wanted to share this
with the rest of the world.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.340
So again, we wrote a paper.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.340
So finally, having built
these three systems,

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.090
we looked back at what
we'd done and said,

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.340
we've got these three
systems that touch

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.885
different aspects of the area.

00:07:13.885 --> 00:07:16.260
It would be really nice if we
took all of the great ideas

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.590
from those and combined them
together into one system.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.580
And so that's what
Cloud Dataflow was.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.050
And Cloud Dataflow really is
two things at a high level.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.540
It's this combined,
unified batch

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.330
plus streaming programming model
and SDKs for actually writing

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:34.990
these pipelines, as well as
a fully managed execution

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.210
engine for running them.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.384
And as our history
has shown, we would

00:07:39.384 --> 00:07:41.300
love to share this with
the rest of the world.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.970
But we finally got it right.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.874
And we finally shared it,
the full system itself.

00:07:45.874 --> 00:07:47.290
And that's what
Cloud Dataflow is.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.930
So you can take this SDK and
download it, write pipelines

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.140
with it, ship them off
to the cloud service,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.600
and it will be executed for
you, which is fantastic.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.480
And just for good measure, we
also wrote a paper about it.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.960
So that's a history of all these
systems that led up to Beam.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.134
But where does Beam fit
into the picture here?

00:08:08.134 --> 00:08:09.800
Well, like I said,
Google Cloud Dataflow

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:10.930
is really these two things.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:15.160
It's this programming model and
SDKs for writing your pipelines

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.410
and this managed service
for executing them.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.140
So when Google, along
with a few partners--

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.570
Talend, Data Artisans,
Cloudera, others--

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.810
decided about a year ago
to create Apache Beam,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:28.786
really what we did was we took
the programming model in SDK

00:08:28.786 --> 00:08:30.910
and donated that to the
Apache Software Foundation.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.380
That's what became Apache Beam.

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.350
And Google Cloud Dataflow itself
remains this managed service

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.320
for executing Apache
Beam pipelines.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.250
And yes, just to be clear, Batch
plus Streaming equals Beam.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:45.130
That's where the name came from.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.770
But what were the
motivations behind Beam?

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.510
Well, there are really four
that are worth touching on here.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:51.970
One is the technology.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.750
All along, we've loved this idea
of sharing awesome technology

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.380
with the rest of the world.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.930
And that's part of the joy
of open source software

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.420
is truly being able to share
not just the technology,

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.870
but everything that
creates that technology.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.820
And that leads then
to communities,

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.610
which is the second, and perhaps
most important, part of this

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.802
is instead of just being
this project that's

00:09:11.802 --> 00:09:14.010
built within Google with
whatever resources Google is

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.300
able to throw at it, with
whatever ideas Googlers may

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.522
have, now it's
developed externally

00:09:18.522 --> 00:09:20.230
with the rest of the
world, incorporating

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:21.760
a diversity of ideas.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.530
We can basically
enlarge the number

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.600
of people that are
actively working on it

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.540
and really push forward the
state of the art in ways

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:29.800
that it never could otherwise.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.950
Just look at the amazing
diversity within the Apache

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:37.030
Hadoop ecosystem and
what's happened there.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.160
Thirdly is the
portability aspect.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.200
We really think that
Beam has a chance

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.020
to be the programmatic
equivalent of what SQL is

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.630
to declarative data processing.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.580
So you can write your
pipeline once in Beam.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.664
And then you can choose to
run it wherever makes sense.

00:09:51.664 --> 00:09:53.080
You can choose to
run it on Spark.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.496
Or you can run it
on Flink, or you

00:09:54.496 --> 00:09:55.780
can run it on Cloud Dataflow.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:57.200
The core concepts are the same.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.880
And also, it's a chance for
us to draw a line in the sand

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.990
and not just give you a lowest
common denominator of what you

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.870
could write for a
pipeline, but really say,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:09.850
this is the state of the art,
this is what things should be,

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:14.020
and then try to help ensure
that runners everywhere

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.330
move up to that high standard.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.240
And lastly, of course, there's
a business reason for Google

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.970
for participating in Beam.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.621
The more people we have
running Beam pipelines,

00:10:23.621 --> 00:10:25.120
the more people
there are that might

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.780
want to run it on Google Cloud.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.750
And our goal as Cloud
Dataflow developers

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:30.400
is to make Dataflow
the absolute best

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.590
place to run Beam pipelines.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.271
But then wearing our
Beam hats, we just

00:10:35.271 --> 00:10:37.270
want to make sure that
Beam is the absolute best

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:39.103
way to write pipelines
in general, no matter

00:10:39.103 --> 00:10:40.310
where you run them.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:43.720
So with that, I've talked about
the history and the motivations

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:44.350
behind Beam.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.042
So I'm going to hand
off to Thomas now.

00:10:46.042 --> 00:10:47.500
And he's going to
talk a little bit

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.274
about the mechanics of bringing
the Beam project to life.

00:10:51.274 --> 00:10:53.410
THOMAS GROH: Thank you.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:56.470
So like Tyler said,
I'm going to be talking

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:06.290
about how we took the the
Java SDK for Cloud Dataflow

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.450
and taken that and our
development process

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.080
and moved all of this to the
Beam project and the Apache

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:16.670
Software Foundation
and what that's

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.510
changed with how we
develop and the process

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:24.630
and what we've actually had to
implement to make that happen.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:28.980
So I'm going to start at
the first Beam pull request.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:35.060
So this is taking all of the
SDK for Java for Dataflow

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.420
and dropping it into
the Beam repository.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.320
And as you may be
able to tell, we

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.570
came here with a
bit of history--

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:44.080
more than 1,500 commits.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.400
We're adding
165,000 [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.300
And this is all after
we've taken a lot of effort

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:52.700
to tease out a lot of the
Dataflow-specific parts

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.230
of the system.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:56.210
But that's not all.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:58.790
We still need to
integrate runners

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.760
that are provided
by Cloudera and data

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.500
artisans for Spark and Flink.

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.720
We still need to reorganize
all of our directories,

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.210
because we built in a lot of
platform-specific assumptions

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.750
when we think we're always
going to be running on TCP.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.300
And we need to just get
Dataflow out of the name.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.180
It's everywhere.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:15.920
It's in all our packages.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:17.045
It's in all our namespaces.

00:12:17.045 --> 00:12:19.490
And we need to move
that, put it in Beam.

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:21.560
And these are all pretty
significant backward

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:23.000
compatible changes.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.870
So anyone who's jumping ship
immediately is going to break.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.489
And while we're doing
all of these changes

00:12:29.489 --> 00:12:31.280
and we're breaking all
of our current users

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.571
who are trying to jump ship
and we're moving everything

00:12:33.571 --> 00:12:36.890
to Beam, we're
also, as engineers,

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:39.890
parachuting into this new
distributed community.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.470
And when you're working
in distributed community,

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:45.670
it's very, very different than
working in a local community.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:49.220
When you're working locally,
you can wander 10 desks down,

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.440
grab a coworker,
grab a whiteboard,

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.720
and just go at it
until both of you

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:55.670
agree on what
you're trying to do.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:57.500
When you're in a
distributed community

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:59.570
and the person is
10 time zones away,

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:01.550
that's basically impossible.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.430
So we've had to develop a new
kind of distributive process

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:07.970
that includes this whole
set of contributors

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:10.220
and puts everyone
on equal footing.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.740
And that's meant we
put a lot of work

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.510
into working on the
mailing list first.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:16.370
If you're not working
on the mailing list,

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:17.453
you're not really working.

00:13:17.453 --> 00:13:20.180
It doesn't really count.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.310
And that's built
a lot of process

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:24.570
where we have documentation.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.714
We built designs, and
we proposed designs.

00:13:26.714 --> 00:13:28.130
And then months
or years later, we

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.680
can look back at those
designs and we can say, well,

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:31.670
why did we do this?

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:32.780
What was the context?

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:36.020
And we can pull all
of that back out.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:39.280
But all of the stuff we
dropped and we contributed

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:42.620
has a lot of that
history behind it.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:44.846
And as a result, it's
really difficult for us

00:13:44.846 --> 00:13:46.220
to say, well, why
did we do this,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.980
because that's all
from years and years

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:51.050
of internal experience.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.810
So it's not really easy
for us to share it.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:59.720
So we've combated this
by writing blog posts.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.180
We've taken things we've
determined internally

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:05.692
like style guides and
our design principles,

00:14:05.692 --> 00:14:07.650
and we brought those to
the community and said,

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:09.530
here, we have
experience doing this.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:10.710
We think these are great.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.376
And then we've worked
with the community

00:14:12.376 --> 00:14:15.290
to publish that kind of design
principles and those style

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.180
guides alongside
some of the more

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:18.830
mechanical parts
of contribution,

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:21.020
like here's how I
push code, here's

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:24.460
how I think about
the code I'm writing.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:29.110
But now we've got a mailing
list and a community

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.130
and a repository.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:31.880
And the repository's
filled with code.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.450
But all that code is
really mostly still

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:37.360
just the Dataflow SDK for Java.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:41.500
And the vision of Beam is
that we have a single model.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.820
And that model works
across languages.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.150
And that model is extensible.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:49.480
And it's a platform for
other people to build on.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:52.120
But when we have just
the Dataflow SDK,

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:54.500
that's not so much the case.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:57.430
So when we want to say, well,
we've got this Beam model,

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.990
then we need to
really codify, well,

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:02.140
what is the Beam model really?

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.600
And we've done this
by building a couple

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:06.550
of new things like
the Runner API

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:09.670
and the Fun API, which are kind
of an extensible core which

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.710
you can build new
SDKs on and which

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.470
you can build new runners on.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:17.560
And they're the extension points
for what Beam as a project is.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.620
It's not just a
single implementation

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:21.820
of our programming model.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:22.990
It's the model.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:27.700
And all of these implementations
build on top of that.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:28.890
And then we've got a vision.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.056
And we've got our code base.

00:15:30.056 --> 00:15:32.970
And we've got all these
features that we're developing.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:34.750
And we've got a
bunch of contributors

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.730
who've managed to start
building on top of this.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:41.830
But beyond that, when you've
got a repository and all

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:45.320
these cool ideas in it,
what you've really got

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.600
is a bunch of text
files in a repository.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.490
And they might be
impressive text files,

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.440
but they're still
just text files.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.140
So we've built
the infrastructure

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:57.770
to enable these contributors
to go, write test, build

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:01.100
their code, run
their code, and try

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.280
to make sure the community
gets access to everything

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:06.190
at every point in this process.

00:16:06.190 --> 00:16:07.730
Designs go on the mailing list.

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:09.470
And the community reviews them.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:12.780
Code goes on GitHub, and
the community reviews it.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:15.140
And then we've also
brought along the culture

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:18.110
of testing and making sure we
know what our code is doing

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.430
and what's happening.

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:25.810
And then because Beam is
this thing that we don't just

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:27.650
want to run, but we
want it to run well

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.890
and we want it to
get better over time,

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:33.400
and we're in the
process right now

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:38.650
of building benchmarking tools
and performance testing tools,

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:40.180
again, for the entire community.

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:44.390
So it's, again, not one
implementation, but all of us.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:47.680
And with this infrastructure
and this extensible platform,

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:50.170
we've seen a lot of
contribution from the community

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:52.990
and new people getting
involved and adding things

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:56.800
like I/O connectors, adding new
runners, adding new libraries.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:59.920
And the runner authors
have been really valuable,

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:03.620
because runners are doing a lot
of development by themselves.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.790
They're saying, we've
got this cool new idea.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.329
We're going to bring it in.

00:17:07.329 --> 00:17:09.280
And then they come to
us on the mailing list.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:12.869
And they're like, we're
trying to do this.

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:15.040
Why don't we put
it in the model?

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.460
And what that means is
that we're all together,

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:20.609
getting better, not
just individually

00:17:20.609 --> 00:17:24.300
we've got this cool new thing
and it only works on Spark,

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:27.089
or it only works on Flink,
or it only works on Dataflow.

00:17:27.089 --> 00:17:29.100
We're saying, build it once.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:30.340
Run it anywhere.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:33.850
It's just going to
get better over time.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:35.850
And then we've just also
got a diverse community

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:37.530
who has their own perspectives.

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:40.172
They see how Beam is used,
how they want to use Beam,

00:17:40.172 --> 00:17:41.130
and they bring it back.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.320
And they say, well,
I want to contribute.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.000
I want to do the thing.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.420
And it's very exciting to see
new people come in and say,

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:49.530
I've got this great idea.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.890
I'm trying to get this
new thing to work here.

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:56.220
I want to work with
you and develop that.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:59.490
And Jesse has been with
us for a pretty long time.

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.710
And he's one of these
community members who come in

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.370
and said, well, here's
how I see Beam being used.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.320
And I'm going to
give you to him now.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:15.960
JESSE ANDERSON: So why
was I looking for Beam

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:17.440
before I knew about it?

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:20.052
When Frances was asking of
you about why you're here,

00:18:20.052 --> 00:18:22.010
hopefully you're thinking
about the same things

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.718
and you're seeing some
of the same things

00:18:23.718 --> 00:18:26.050
that I am, because I
teach pretty extensively.

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:29.320
And I was seeing some issues
out in the big data community

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.320
that I was looking
for a solution.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:35.076
So there is a picture of me.

00:18:35.076 --> 00:18:36.700
You can see that I
have a new body now.

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:37.908
It's kind of like "Futurama."

00:18:37.908 --> 00:18:39.640
But there I have that new body.

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:44.320
And that was me walking
around Strata San Jose 2016--

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:45.640
about a year ago--

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:49.114
asking everybody I knew that
I was saying, I'm seeing this.

00:18:49.114 --> 00:18:49.780
I'm seeing this.

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:51.010
I'm seeing this.

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.120
And I'll go through the
things that I was seeing.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:56.890
But I was really trying
to find a solution.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:58.310
I'm seeing these issues.

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:01.090
And you're probably seeing
those issues right now.

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:03.550
And then I bumped into
this guy at Google

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.560
named William Vambenepe.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:10.120
And I said to William,
everyone's having to rewrite.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:11.740
How many of you are
sick of rewriting

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:14.740
every time you went
from maybe MapReduce

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:18.070
or you went from one
framework to another?

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:19.270
Are you tired of that?

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:20.620
I know I am.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:23.800
This is absolute
waste of our time.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.650
And then I don't know
about you, but since I

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:28.570
trained in big data,
I have to learn

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:31.950
every single one of these APIs.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:33.790
There's value for
me to doing that.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:35.890
There isn't value
for you to do that.

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:39.130
You trying to learn every single
one of these new APIs that

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:42.800
don't work with each
other, that's a big issue.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:45.070
That's a total
waste of your time.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:48.580
And then I put my CTO hat
on-- hopefully you got enough

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.430
of that during the keynotes
today of CTO speak--

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:53.140
well, here's my CTO speak.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:54.370
Guess what?

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.590
Some of these projects
are going to die.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.650
There is not enough room
for all these frameworks

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:01.390
to completely live.

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:03.120
There is not enough
market share.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:06.535
Inevitably, we're already seeing
some of these products dying.

00:20:06.535 --> 00:20:08.200
And you know what's
going to happen?

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.000
Those companies are going
to be stuck in the lurch.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.310
They're either going to have
to prop that product up,

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.900
or they're going to
have to move again.

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:15.700
And guess what?

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.820
When you make that case to
your CTO or your manager

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.960
and you say, oh,
guess what, we just

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:24.790
rewrote to this one last
year, that project died.

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.680
We're going to have
to go over here.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.680
Those are not the
kind of conversations

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.930
you want to have with your CTO.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.540
So I said, what
are people doing,

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:36.880
because I knew I
was going around.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:38.500
Everybody was
saying, I don't know.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.090
I'm seeing these things, too.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.220
Or they may have
been seeing them.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:45.910
And then William
said, Apache Beam.

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:47.860
And I said, OK,
well, what's that?

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:49.900
Give me some more information.

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.220
And there we have it.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:56.260
So here's what I want you to
take away from this session.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:59.290
If we're learning
framework-specific APIs,

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:01.630
every time a new
framework comes out,

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:03.250
we have to completely change.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:05.400
And they completely
change their existing API.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:08.330
That doesn't create
any value at all.

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:10.300
Have you ever seen
an SEC filing where

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:14.290
somebody said, hey, we moved
from MapReduce over to Apache

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:15.100
Spark.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.320
You ever seen an SEC
filing that says that?

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:18.340
No.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:21.580
Companies do not care about
what framework you are using.

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:25.300
They care about what business
value you're creating.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.870
And if we can have a
system like Beam that

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:31.430
allows you to create that
business value faster,

00:21:31.430 --> 00:21:35.300
better, not having to rewrite
code all the time, guess what?

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:37.854
It's a total waste of
your time to do rewrites.

00:21:37.854 --> 00:21:39.520
It's a total waste
of your time to learn

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.320
all these new framework APIs.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.350
There's just a whole
bunch of problems there.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:47.770
So it looks like a lot of you
were familiar with the big data

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:48.950
ecosystem.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:52.180
And this is a diagram I
often share of the big data

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:53.450
ecosystem.

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:55.900
So looking at this, we see
all sorts of different things.

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:57.880
We see Real-Time clusters.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:21:59.680
We see Pub/Sub clusters.

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:03.010
We see Real-Time SQL
clusters, Hadoop clusters,

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:04.510
NoSQL clusters.

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:07.210
And you see all these edge
things around consuming them,

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:08.800
working with them.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:12.760
So here's the problem with this.

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:15.620
How many different APIs
do you see on this slide?

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:23.290
There's 20, maybe 30
different APIs on this slide.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.200
And you know what's the
fun kicker about this?

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.980
The API for that
Real-Time cluster

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.440
to reach into that Pub/Sub
cluster is actually different.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:35.950
So there's an API that
that Pub/Sub cluster has

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:37.510
when you directly access it.

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.360
Oh, and then there's a brand
new API when, let's say,

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:43.180
Apache Spark accesses
Apache Kafka.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:45.190
That's a brand
new separate API--

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:46.870
separate one you have to learn.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:48.430
Who's excited about this?

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:50.720
Lots of learning, lots
of value being created.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:53.150
So these are some of the
things that I was seeing.

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:56.380
Here is a visualization of this
where we're looking at this

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:58.000
and we're saying,
oh, my goodness,

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.220
there are way too
many different APIs,

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:02.874
way too many different
abstractions in these APIs.

00:23:02.874 --> 00:23:04.540
Wouldn't it be great
if we had something

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:07.120
that could allow us
to go through these--

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:10.210
not have to learn
every single one?

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:13.390
So let me talk about
why I personally

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:14.840
am excited about Beam.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.052
And this leads into my journey
of becoming a committer.

00:23:18.052 --> 00:23:20.260
But I want you to understand,
and hopefully as you're

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:23.950
sitting here, this is
why I put the effort,

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.190
I put my time into it
to become a committer.

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:30.910
And hopefully, these are
issues that you're seeing now

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:33.160
or will see in the
future that I think

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:35.860
Beam is correcting and fixing.

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:37.720
One of them-- the
slide we just saw--

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:39.610
we saw 20 or 30 different APIs.

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:43.130
You know how many APIs you'd
have to learn with Beam?

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:43.770
One.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.460
We've got one API
to rule them all.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:46.180
There we go.

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:47.740
Just one API to learn.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:51.130
And you can deploy that
API onto Apache Spark.

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:53.740
You can do that on the Dataflow.

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:58.810
Another big one-- so
putting my CTO hat back on,

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:00.855
I can move between
frameworks now.

00:24:00.855 --> 00:24:02.230
You don't know
how important that

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:06.220
is until you've ever had to move
frameworks and do a reroute.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:08.200
That's actually
pretty important to be

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:09.910
able to sell this internally.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:11.440
If you're going to
say, by the way,

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:13.990
we just finished that
Apache Spark rewrite,

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:17.530
or whatever rewrite we did,
and now when we go to Flink,

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:19.810
we're going to have
to do another rewrite,

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:21.370
I don't really want to do that.

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:22.810
We'll obviously have to retest.

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.730
We'd have to QA, perf
test-- all that fun stuff.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:30.160
But it ideally won't be a bunch
of development time involved

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:32.146
in that.

00:24:32.146 --> 00:24:33.520
Out of curiosity,
how many of you

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:37.030
are familiar with Spark
Streaming and Spark APIs?

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:41.530
So hopefully, you've looked at
that from an API point of view.

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:44.080
Beam is actually
the most unified API

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:47.170
I've seen, in terms
of batch and stream.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:52.960
If you've worked with the
streaming API and the batch

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:55.930
API, they're somewhat similar.

00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:58.150
But they're different
enough where you actually

00:24:58.150 --> 00:24:59.130
have to learn--

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:00.820
I just wrote a class on this--

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.860
you actually have to
learn, here are the nuances

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:04.720
to the streaming API.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.330
Here are the nuances
to the batch API.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.680
It's actually not as coherent
and not as similar in API

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:12.970
as you'd like to think.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:18.070
And that's a big issue, because
we would ideally want something

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:20.110
like Beam, as Tyler showed.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:20.890
It's Beam.

00:25:20.890 --> 00:25:23.281
It's batch, and it's streaming.

00:25:23.281 --> 00:25:25.030
And when you start to
go through that API,

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:28.180
you'll actually see, oh, OK,
this is how they do that.

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:31.600
I don't have to write very
different code for my streaming

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:35.020
versus by my batch analytics.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:39.200
Another one-- unified
API to all the ecosystem.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:40.930
So that slide that
I showed before,

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:43.150
if we were to do that
with Beam, we'd have one

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:46.450
API into Kafka, whether
we're running on Spark.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.990
Whether we're running on this
or that, it's that single API.

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:52.940
Or if you're going to go into
some other ecosystem project,

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:54.407
it's all that single API.

00:25:54.407 --> 00:25:55.990
And that's where a
lot of our effort's

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:58.310
going into in the project.

00:25:58.310 --> 00:26:00.730
Once again, CTO hat on--

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:03.340
that risk mitigation,
it's very, very important.

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:06.130
If you're going to talk
to your suits back at work

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.560
and say, hey, I
heard about Beam,

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:11.260
you talk about risk
mitigation of not

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:14.260
putting all our eggs in one
basket and being able to move.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:17.690
And then they'll
understand it much better.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:20.430
So these are some ways in
which I've contributed.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:22.550
If these are helpful to
you, I won't go into them

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:23.480
in great depth.

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.160
I wrote some positioning pieces.

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:28.520
Once again, if you back
to work and you say, hey--

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:30.470
the suits and you talk
to the suits once more,

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.780
and you want a
positioning piece of this

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:35.990
is why somebody should
actually be looking at Beam

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:38.750
or a company should be looking
at Beam, you can go there.

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.600
I've written two
pieces on positioning.

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:43.790
I also brought some
extensive point of view

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:45.800
from training
where I've actually

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.170
written a class on Apache Beam.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:51.440
I've done some pretty
significant evangelism on Beam.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.500
Tyler and I spoke
at Strata New York.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:59.750
I'll be going to QCon Sao Paulo
later on, talking about Beam,

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:01.670
talking to people about Beam.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.130
If you want to see
some code, this talk

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.530
will obviously be
pretty devoid of code.

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:08.300
But there are some
more example code.

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:12.535
If you go to that link, it
will send you on to my GitHub.

00:27:12.535 --> 00:27:13.910
And then there is
the interacting

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:15.530
with a team as a non-Googler.

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:17.510
So this is an interesting
point of view.

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:19.850
That's why they put
the token non-Googler

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:21.440
up here on the
stage, because they

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.410
wanted you to hear from
somebody who isn't from Google.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:28.020
So ideally, you won't think I've
drank Kool-Aid or something.

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:30.170
And so what do I do?

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:31.310
Why do I like the team?

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:33.050
What's it like
interacting with the team?

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:35.100
They're over there, so
I'll kind of go like this

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:37.020
so I can't see them.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.820
But what are they
like to interact with?

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.690
And I actually really enjoy
interacting with the Beam Team

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:46.520
where we've all gotten
along very well.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:49.700
It's actually been a great
journey to talk about.

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:51.710
So I'm actually going
to talk about that

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:53.090
with my first commit.

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:57.170
So if you're thinking that
from maybe a little bit

00:27:57.170 --> 00:27:59.702
of trepidation or
maybe, I don't know

00:27:59.702 --> 00:28:01.160
about interacting
with them, that's

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:02.285
kind of how I started that.

00:28:02.285 --> 00:28:04.446
I was like, all right,
let me dig into this.

00:28:04.446 --> 00:28:05.570
Let me throw out something.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:07.190
Let me try some
code and see what

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:09.470
that's like,
because I personally

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:12.410
have contributed to several
different open source projects.

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:13.700
I've contributed to Hadoop.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:16.430
I've contributed to HBase--

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:18.590
quite a few-- even Kafka.

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:19.880
So here's a commit.

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:23.780
This is my first commit that I
added some type descriptors in.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:25.850
And it was pretty simple thing.

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:28.190
But it was something
that I saw in the API

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:29.750
that hey, this is really good.

00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:32.600
This would be something that
would be interesting to add in.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:34.320
It's going to save
some lines of code.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:36.380
It's going to save some
conceptual overhead

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:38.660
of trying to understand Beam.

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:41.450
So those three lightning
bolts was boom,

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:43.490
that the interactions
with the Beam

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:46.310
Team-- if you want
to go and read those,

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:49.790
it's some good bedtime reading
to read GitHub interactions

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:52.190
about, pushes and pulls.

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.481
And then Ken from the Beam Team
said, hey, looks good to me,

00:28:55.481 --> 00:28:56.480
I'm going to merge this.

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:58.130
That was my first commit.

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.260
It was pretty painless actually.

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:05.020
If you've ever done a pull or
a push into-- a pull request

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:06.072
into GitHub--

00:29:06.072 --> 00:29:06.780
it's not too bad.

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:08.010
It's not terrible.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:11.700
So when Frances was talking
about, hey, find either a JIRA

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:14.690
or find something--
look in the API.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:16.250
I saw a few people
with your hands

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:18.890
up that your Dataflow SDK users.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:20.792
Maybe there is
something you'd say,

00:29:20.792 --> 00:29:22.250
there was always
something I really

00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:24.980
wish was part of Dataflow SDK.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:27.200
Well, on the Beam
side, we've actually

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:30.200
had some people come in and say,
there is this thing I wanted.

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:31.910
Boom, there's the
push that they did.

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:34.010
And they said, here,
I've already written this

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:36.230
and contributed it.

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:38.810
So eventually, I
became a committer.

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:41.170
And this is the
actual announcement.

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:42.590
I was one of the
first committers.

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:45.290
And you can see that
they're at the bottom.

00:29:45.290 --> 00:29:46.090
There's Thomas.

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:49.250
Thomas was made a committer
at the same time as me.

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:54.930
So that wasn't a
significant amount of time.

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:57.620
But it was an amount of effort.

00:29:57.620 --> 00:29:59.660
It was an amount of
effort that I decided

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.610
and I realized, yes,
this is something

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:02.660
I actually want to do.

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:05.660
This is something
that I see Beam

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:07.760
doing what I want it to do.

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:13.430
So going back to that initial
talk that I had with William,

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:16.280
some of the things that
stuck out to me about Beam

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:21.470
that I hadn't heard before is
that it wasn't somebody's idea,

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:24.710
it wasn't somebody's theory,
and it wasn't somebody's PhD

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:25.820
thesis.

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:30.510
This was code running
in production.

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:32.390
There's a very big
difference between that.

00:30:32.390 --> 00:30:35.000
If you're coming from
the open source community

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.950
and you've seen
some of that, you

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:39.500
might have seen
somebody's PhD thesis

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:42.260
and tried to use
that in production.

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:43.260
You're sitting down.

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.730
You may fall down otherwise.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:47.480
PhD theses don't [INAUDIBLE]
well in production.

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:49.370
Oh, my goodness, it happens.

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:53.240
But here, what we have
is we have real code.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:55.730
As Thomas showed,
there was a lot of code

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:56.960
that was contributed.

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:58.310
It was mature code.

00:30:58.310 --> 00:30:59.960
It was code running production.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:02.450
And I thought, OK, this
is exactly what I can do.

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:06.200
I can get behind a project
that's really getting started.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:07.850
It actually has
some miles on it.

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:09.184
It's proven in production.

00:31:09.184 --> 00:31:10.725
There are some other
things around it

00:31:10.725 --> 00:31:12.680
that we're going to
expand on and improve on.

00:31:12.680 --> 00:31:15.650
But the core of it-- the
really, really hard parts--

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:19.410
are pretty much done, and
they're pretty much proven.

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:22.770
So when you look at products
and projects out there,

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:26.070
that's a really important thing
to look at in open source is,

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:28.500
is there miles on it production?

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:30.660
Are there people using it?

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:34.020
That's a good metric when you
put your CTO hat on and say,

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:37.410
should I use this
technology or not?

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.910
So here's a graph that
I wanted to show you.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:44.820
And the thing I like about
this graph is a few things.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:47.130
So the blue line is Google.

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:52.800
So these are contributions
to, now, Apache Beam,

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:56.910
to the left of the incubation
period, Dataflow SDK.

00:31:56.910 --> 00:32:00.840
So to the left of that, we
have the pre-incubation period,

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:02.310
mostly Google.

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:06.690
But now what we're seeing
is after the Apache donation

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:11.100
there on the right side
of that yellow line,

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:12.720
we're seeing a
significant uptick.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:14.889
And these are unique
contributors per month.

00:32:14.889 --> 00:32:16.680
So I'm obviously one
of those contributors.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.170
I usually try to contribute
something at least

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.100
every month, if not more.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:24.600
And so what we're seeing
here is a significant uptick

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.130
of people like you.

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:30.000
It really warmed my heart
to actually look out and see

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.520
how many of you
said, I'm looking

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:32.520
to become a contributor.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:34.290
If you didn't see me,
I clapped, because I

00:32:34.290 --> 00:32:36.420
would love to see
you all participate,

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:39.360
because it's a great
community to be in.

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:41.490
And what we're seeing
is the manifestation

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:43.530
of a great community.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:45.330
Actually, hey, there's
a chart for that.

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:48.660
And this chart is showing
we've got some upticks in terms

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:51.510
of people contributing, people
continuing to contribute,

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.640
people actually
creating things that are

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.710
very interesting about them.

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:59.040
So here we have
this gradual uptick.

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:02.250
And that uptick is
obviously not from Google.

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:05.220
And we also have Google
continuing to contribute.

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.020
So that's really
some of the things

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.000
that I look for
in open source is,

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:10.680
is there a commercial
company behind it

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:11.820
with a vested interest?

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:19.560
As he talked about, Google
does have a vested interest.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.810
And that's what
Tyler talked about--

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:23.370
that Google has
that vested interest

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:26.760
in continuing to promote
this, continuing to run this.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:28.710
And then there's
other people like me

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:31.710
who have a vested interest
in building Beam and making

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:34.740
Beam something even better.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:37.370
So I wanted to
give you some code,

00:33:37.370 --> 00:33:41.550
just in case you got bored
and were looking for code.

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:42.800
There's this thing.

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:46.772
If you've ever been
to big data summits

00:33:46.772 --> 00:33:48.230
or looked at big
data technologies,

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:49.470
there's word count.

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.620
Well, there's also
this other thing of,

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:54.500
how small can you
make your word count?

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:56.630
So I actually wrote
some word counts.

00:33:56.630 --> 00:34:00.052
And I said, this word
count is too big.

00:34:00.052 --> 00:34:02.260
And it was like the "Goldilocks
and the Three Bears."

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:05.540
So I made word count
a little bit smaller,

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:08.419
and then I made it as
small as I possibly could.

00:34:08.419 --> 00:34:09.710
So this is actually word count.

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:12.020
This is a word count that
you could run right there

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.570
on Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:34:14.570 --> 00:34:17.850
This word count is pretty small.

00:34:17.850 --> 00:34:20.060
These are actually
a few of the commits

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:21.650
that I've done personally.

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:23.300
The regex-- I don't
know if you've ever

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:26.960
done a regular expression
in, let's say, Spark code.

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:29.000
More than likely, you
did it inefficiently.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.042
Let me just tell you that.

00:34:31.042 --> 00:34:32.750
If you don't know what
I'm talking about,

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:35.120
come out to me afterward and
I'll tell you exactly why.

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:37.310
But more than likely,
you did not [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:39.560
incredibly inefficiently.

00:34:39.560 --> 00:34:40.489
So I created one.

00:34:40.489 --> 00:34:43.370
I said, here's an efficient
one, because we often

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:45.440
do regular expressions.

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:47.060
And then we have our count.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:48.960
And then there's this
other two string.

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:53.540
So now we have a built-in way
of taking whatever object you

00:34:53.540 --> 00:34:54.170
have--

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:57.770
let's save out the strings of
it and write that to the file.

00:34:57.770 --> 00:34:59.370
So these are just some examples.

00:34:59.370 --> 00:35:01.730
These are obviously
pretty simple examples.

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:03.260
But these are some
examples I hope

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:05.990
to hold out to you as, this
is how you personally could

00:35:05.990 --> 00:35:07.670
start contributing to Beam.

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:10.340
If you're already
a Dataflow SDK user

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:13.680
or you're looking through
the API and saying,

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:15.440
hey, I'd really love
to see something

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:19.797
like this or something like
that in Beam, you can do it.

00:35:19.797 --> 00:35:20.380
You can do it.

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:21.320
You can actually go through.

00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:23.360
You could look at some
of my previous commits,

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:26.390
see that they're interesting.

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:29.960
So now we're going to hand
it back over to Frances.

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:32.129
She's going to talk about
what's next for Beam.

00:35:36.041 --> 00:35:37.710
FRANCES PERRY: Thanks, Jesse.

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:38.210
All right.

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:40.210
So I wanted to talk a
little bit about the state

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:45.280
of the project, where we're
at, what Beam's next steps are.

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:47.410
So I think many of you who
know the Apache Software

00:35:47.410 --> 00:35:49.510
Foundation know
that projects start

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:51.730
in a phase called incubation.

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:53.410
This is basically a
bootstrapping phase

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:54.910
where the project
is showing that it

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.090
can act in the Apache way.

00:35:57.090 --> 00:36:00.400
The Apache way is very
much community before code.

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:02.950
You're building an
open meritocracy where

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:05.570
anyone can come and develop.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:11.780
So Beam graduated-- oops,
those went the wrong way.

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.700
There we go.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:16.590
So Beam graduated
in December, which

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:19.005
basically is a statement about
the health of the project.

00:36:19.005 --> 00:36:22.260
So Beam is now a top level
project at the Apache Software

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:25.170
Foundation moving forwards.

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:26.850
And along with that
great community

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:30.430
that we've built, though, we
really need a solid technology.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:32.550
So one of the large
focuses going forward

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:35.250
is this portability
structure and infrastructure

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:36.930
that Thomas alluded to.

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:40.220
This is what lets users mix
and match the different SDKs

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.900
and the runners so they can
really choose the language that

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:45.360
meets their needs in
the runtime environment

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:47.662
that they're currently after.

00:36:47.662 --> 00:36:49.120
One of the other
things we're doing

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:51.280
is continuing to treat
Beam as the glue that

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.230
integrates other projects.

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:56.560
So we'll be adding support for
additional storage formats,

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:00.160
new DSLs that let you write
Beam-style pipelines in maybe

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:01.480
SQL, Scala.

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:03.730
There's a lot of work out
there, because again, people

00:37:03.730 --> 00:37:06.130
want to be able to use the
technology and the power

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:09.790
it brings in the environment
that's familiar to them.

00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:12.310
And finally, one of the
things we're committed to

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:15.190
is clear promises about
backwards and compatible API

00:37:15.190 --> 00:37:16.060
changes.

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:18.730
We've all seen what a pain it
is when the API you're using

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:20.980
is constantly
changing underfoot.

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:23.500
So the Beam project is moving
towards its first stable

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:26.530
release where we'll be
able to use Sem Versioning

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:28.882
and make some very
clear promises.

00:37:28.882 --> 00:37:31.090
So those discussions are
underway on the mailing list

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:31.600
right now.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.090
Looking like maybe
early April, we'll

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:37.170
hit that milestone
as a community.

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:39.900
Now again, all this
technology that we're doing

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:42.780
needs a diverse
and open community.

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.900
And so I really encourage you to
come and start looking at Beam

00:37:45.900 --> 00:37:47.580
and what we've got to offer.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:48.810
So you can go to our website.

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:52.630
We've got a very active user and
dev mailing list you can join.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:55.110
The website has a lot of
information, podcasts,

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:58.320
articles, videos
about the Beam model

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:00.564
that you can learn
in more detail.

00:38:00.564 --> 00:38:01.480
There's a Quick Start.

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:04.145
So if you've got a Flink cluster
in your back pocket right now

00:38:04.145 --> 00:38:05.520
and you'd like to
run Beam on it,

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:08.370
we've got a Quick Start
to help you do that.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:09.790
And we have JIRA issues.

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:11.280
Some of them are
tag with Starter.

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:13.410
So feel free to find
an issue that you

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:16.260
think you could tackle and
dive in and start contributing.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:19.180
We really welcome new
voices in the community.

00:38:19.180 --> 00:38:20.982
You'll find if you
post a welcome message

00:38:20.982 --> 00:38:22.440
and just introduce
yourself, you'll

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:25.410
get a huge bunch of people just
plus oneing that, welcoming

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:28.350
you to join us.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.367
Later in the afternoon,
in a couple hours,

00:38:30.367 --> 00:38:31.950
there's another talk
on Beam that I'll

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:34.500
be giving that focuses
more on the technology.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.200
So there, what I'll do is
introduce more of the details

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:40.020
behind this unified
model and how that works

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:43.620
and also give a demo of the
same Beam pipeline running

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:46.797
on Cloud Dataflow, Apache
Spark, and Apache Flink

00:38:46.797 --> 00:38:48.630
to show you that this
portability that we're

00:38:48.630 --> 00:38:52.315
building out is really
coming to fruition.

00:38:52.315 --> 00:38:54.690
So with that, I think I'll
invite the other presenters up

00:38:54.690 --> 00:38:55.190
here.

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:56.670
We'd love to take
some questions.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:39:00.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

